      en: "Khartoum is the capital of the Republic of Sudan, strategically positioned at the confluence of the White Nile and the Blue Nile, a location famously known as Al-Mogran. The city was established in 1821 under the rule of Ibrahim Pasha as a military outpost for the Egyptian-Ottoman administration and rapidly evolved into a regional trade hub. Historically, it gained global prominence during the Siege of Khartoum in 1885, when Mahdist forces overcame the British-Egyptian garrison. Today, Khartoum, together with its sister cities Omdurman and Khartoum Bahri, forms a massive tripartite metropolis that serves as the political, economic, and cultural heart of Sudan. The architecture presents a stark contrast between British-era colonial structures and modern glass-fronted developments along the Nile's embankments. Geographically, the city lies in a semi-arid zone, resulting in extremely hot summers and sparse annual rainfall. As a central transportation node, Khartoum connects the northern regions with the resource-rich areas of the south and west. Despite ongoing political challenges, the city remains a significant educational center, home to institutions like the University of Khartoum, founded in 1902.",
